framework:

  System.Text.Json                          8.0.5       MIT

Apache-2.0 dependencies: the licence text is in the LICENSE file beside this one,
which is the same text this product is offered under.

MIT and MIT-0 dependencies: permissive, attribution-only, and the attribution is
this list. MIT-0 does not require even that; UnitsNet is named anyway.

System.Xml.XPath.XmlDocument 4.3.0 predates the .NET Foundation's move to MIT and
carries the Microsoft .NET Library License, which is a redistribution licence
rather than an open-source one. It permits distribution of the assembly as part of
a program of this kind. It is the only package in the shipped set under those
terms, and it arrives transitively through the SAF SDK rather than by any choice
made here.


EPPlus 4.5.3.3 -- LGPL-3.0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Copyright (c) EPPlus Software AB and contributors.
  https://www.nuget.org/packages/EPPlus/4.5.3.3

EPPlus is the Excel reader behind the SAF adapter, and it is the one dependency
here whose licence imposes an obligation on this product rather than merely
permitting its use.

Note the version of the licence. The package declares LGPL-3.0 -- not the LGPL-2.1
a reader might reasonably assume -- and carries the text as `lgpl-3.0.txt`. That
text ships beside the binary, copied verbatim out of the package and unaltered, as

    licenses/LGPL-3.0.txt

EPPlus is consumed as an unmodified assembly delivered by NuGet. No source file in
this repository references an EPPlus type through anything other than that
assembly's public API, no part of EPPlus has been altered, and no EPPlus source is
included here or in any distributed artefact.

A CONSUMER MAY REPLACE EPPlus.dll WITH THEIR OWN BUILD OF EPPlus 4.5.3.3.

That freedom -- to relink this product against a modified version of the LGPL
library -- is what makes the arrangement lawful for Apache-2.0 code, and it is why
the licence text and this notice have to travel with the binary rather than live
only in the repository. Dropping a replacement EPPlus.dll of the same assembly
version into the directory beside femex.exe is sufficient: nothing in this product
prevents it, and nothing about that assembly is signed or version-locked at run
time.

The reference in griffel-femex.Adapters.Saf.csproj is pinned to the exact version
`[4.5.3.3]`, and the bracket is a licence control rather than a build preference.
4.5.3.3 is the last release of EPPlus offered under the LGPL; from version 5
onward EPPlus is licensed under Polyform Noncommercial, which a commercially sold
product may not use without a paid commercial licence. The SAF SDK declares only
EPPlus >= 4.5.3.3, so an unbracketed reference would float, and a transitive bump
would put this product in breach silently, with a green build. The bracket is what
stops that. The reason is recorded here, and not only in the code comment beside
it, because a control whose justification lives inside the repository is one that
nobody holding the binary can see.
